{"name":"Chrysocolla Medium Green","hex":"#006b57","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chrysocolla","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A vitreous silicate mineral that is a minor ore of copper, with the chemical formula (Cu,Al)2H2Si2O5(OH)4·nH2O."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chrysocolla"},{"term":"medium","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The material of the surrounding environment, e.g. solid, liquid, gas, vacuum, or a specific substance such as a solvent.","The materials or empty space through which signals, waves, or forces pass."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of several common sizes to which an item may be manufactured, smaller than a large but larger than a small.","An item labelled or denoted as being that size."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/medium"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chrysocolla","blocks":[{"text":"Chrysocolla ( KRIS-ə-KOL-ə) is a hydrous copper phyllosilicate mineral and mineraloid with the formula Cu2 – xAlx(H2Si2O5)(OH)4⋅nH2O (x < 1) or (Cu, Al)2H2Si2O5(OH)4⋅nH2O)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Chrysocolla-230109.jpg/330px-Chrysocolla-230109.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chrysocolla","wikidata":"Q407485"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg/250px-Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg/500px-Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg/250px-Chrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChrysocolla-Tyrolite-Clinotyrolite-202106.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Chrysocolla-201585.jpg/250px-Chrysocolla-201585.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.or